From: MANET <manet@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] mt76: mt7915: automatic per-peer transmit power

At short range full transmit power can overload a mesh peer's receiver,
and it heats the card for no gain. Lower the data power toward each close
mesh peer automatically, and raise it again as the link weakens, without
costing the link its rate.

Link metric: the signal of a peer's broadcast and management frames
(beacons, mesh action frames, and group data such as BATMAN broadcasts).
A peer sends these at its normal power, outside any per-station limit, so
their signal measures path loss and does not move when the peer lowers
its own data power. Each end sets its cap from the same path loss on its
own, so the two ends cannot chase each other. The peer comes from the raw
RXD WLAN index, taken before group delivery remaps it to the local vif and
checked against the transmitter address where available. Otherwise the
over-air transmitter address (802.11 header or RXD group 4) is looked up
in mac80211's station hash. The translated Ethernet source is never used.
Unicast data (including multicast-to-unicast copies), FCS failures and
monitor-only frames are excluded. Sampling keeps 0017's rules: at most
four samples a second, a ten-second expiry, and one sta_poll_lock for
publication and snapshot.

0017's dual/full choice now uses the same metric. A peer's lowered power
therefore no longer pushes our choice for it toward full. Its -62/-68
thresholds stay: on the bench this signal read 0 to 1 dB above unicast
data on 5 GHz and 1 to 3 dB above on 2.4 GHz.

Cap: with the MANET WM, an associated mesh peer with automatic power and
a two-stream HE link in full or dual mode gets a cap when its signal is
above -48 dBm. The cap falls in 2 dB steps, at most 8 dB below the phy's
aggregate power and never below 10 dBm. Deepening an existing cap needs
another 2 dB of signal. A weaker or stale signal, or disabling the rule,
raises power at once.

Rate guard: a reduction needs two adjacent uncapped windows that agree.
A window lasts at least 5 s, and up to 30 s, to collect 16 tracked data
completions. The two must agree within half an MCS of mean HE MCS and
within 10 percentage points of sampled errors (ACK errors and mode, NSS,
bandwidth or DCM mismatches), with at most 25% errors. That baseline is
frozen while capped. A capped window more than half an MCS below it, or
more than 10 points above its error ratio, clears the cap and schedules a
retry after 5 minutes, doubling to at most an hour. A sparse window keeps
the cap while it collects samples. The 30 s timeout clears the cap
without a penalty, as does 2 s without a status. Ten minutes of good
capped operation resets the backoff. TX statuses carry a generation
stamp in the mt76 TX control block, so statuses from before or during a
change cannot qualify it.

Controls: tx_power_auto (default on) enables the rule. A limit set by
hand with "iw dev <if> station set <MAC> txpwr limit" takes precedence,
and "txpwr auto" hands the peer back. No interface restart, peer message
or daemon is involved. peer_tx_paths also shows each peer's cap, baseline,
window and retry state. At most eight stations are programmed per
watchdog pass, in circular WCID order. With the rule off and no cap left,
the pass skips station iteration. An MCU failure retries an uncapped
table.

The mean-rate guard is not a TCP throughput floor and does not guarantee
every packet's rate. The driver's TXD and beacon paths and earlier WM
tracing show basic frames bypassing per-station limits; radiated power
per frame class has not been measured.

---
